## Benchmark infrastructure (2026-05-17, post smoke-test pass)
================================================================================

Goal: produce baseline-vs-SE cycle/instruction numbers for six PolyBench-MINI
kernels (jacobi-1d, jacobi-2d, mvt, atax, gemm, 2mm) for the dissertation.

### Cycle/instruction counters

- `ips/riscv/tb/core/custom/bench.h` (new) — helpers for RI5CY's PCER counters.
  - `bench_init()`        writes PCER=0xCC0 with bits [cycles, instr] enabled,
                           PCMR=0xCC1 with ACTIVE|SATURATE.
  - `bench_reset_counters()` writes 0 to CSR 0x79F (counter-clear helper).
  - `bench_cycle()` / `bench_instret()` read PCCR0/PCCR1 (CSRs 0x780/0x781).
- Standard `rdcycle`/`rdinstret` are NOT implemented on RI5CY — must use the
  PCER-mapped counters above.

### Results readback path

- Firmware writes `(label, cycles, instrs)` triples into a "results region"
  starting at L2 address `0x1C07E000` (well below heap/stack).
- 32 slots × 12 B = 384 B reserved.
- `BENCH_SENTINEL = 0xFFFFFFFF` written after the last live slot.
- `rtl/tb/tb_pulp.sv` (line ~1080): after the exit-status poll, scans the
  results region via JTAG SBA `readMem()` and `$display`s each slot as
  `[BENCH] slot N label=0xLLLLLLLL cycles=C instrs=I`.

### Build/run

- `ips/riscv/tb/core/Makefile.se_smoke` (modified):
  - `APP ?= se_smoke` selects the top-level C file.
  - `KERNEL ?= all` expands to one or more `-DBENCH_<NAME>` CFLAGS.
    Valid values: `jac1d mvt atax gemm 2mm jac2d all`.
  - Default invocation:
    `make -f Makefile.se_smoke APP=se_bench KERNEL=<n> clean_smoke all sim_fast`

- `ips/riscv/tb/core/custom/se_bench.c` (new, ~570 lines) — six PolyBench
  kernels in one source file, each gated by `#ifdef BENCH_<NAME>`. Per-kernel
  pattern:
    - `<k>_init()`     PolyBench-style data fill
    - `<k>_baseline()` regular C with ld/sw — measured under PCER
    - `<k>_se()`       SE-driven reads via `stream.pop` + regular `sw` for
                       writes — measured under PCER
    - `do_<k>()`       calls both, records two BENCH slots

### SE constraint discovered during bench bring-up

**Re-arm hang.** The read-stream FSM (`read_stream_fsm.sv`) supports
`DONE → ACTIVE` re-arm on a fresh start pulse. This works for small re-arm
counts but hangs deterministically at higher counts:

| Re-arms (passes) | Outcome |
|---|---|
| 1  (Phase C smoke)         | pass |
| 2  (jac1d T=1)             | pass — 240 513 cycles / 13 778 instrs |
| 40 (jac1d T=20, amortized) | indefinite hang |
| 40 (jac1d T=20, full rewrite each pass) | indefinite hang |

The hang style: `stream.pop` blocks forever in EX — the SE never asserts
`pop_valid`, meaning it never entered ACTIVE for the bad re-arm. The exact
threshold between 2 and 40 has not been bisected.

Workaround in the bench: `J1_T = J2_T = 1` (PolyBench-MINI default is 20).
Documented in `se_bench.c` comments and the thesis limitations section.
Hypothesized root cause: residual state in the reuse buffer / AGU counters /
outstanding-AXI tracking that the `DONE → ACTIVE` edge does not clear.
Future-work fix in `read_stream_fsm.sv`: explicit state-clear handshake on
the DONE-edge, or a software-pulsed `RESET_STREAM` bit in `SE_CTRL`.

**Concurrent pop+push hang.** The full SE path (in0 read + out0 write driven
by kernel `stream.pop` and `stream.push` instructions in the same loop) hangs.
Smoke-test Phase F validated all-3-streams concurrency but with MMIO-driven
push (`SE_OUT0_PUSH` register), not the custom instruction. As workaround the
bench uses **SE for reads only**; outputs go through regular `sw`. Documented
in the SE-version function comments. To re-test concurrent pop+push after
the re-arm fix is in place.

