## Dual-stream SE bug — in0+in1 with reuse_dim=1 (2026-05-17)
================================================================================

While bringing up the mvt and atax benchmarks (both use `in0=A row-major,
in1=vector with reuse_dim=1`) the kernel deadlocks in `stream.pop`. Captured
diagnostic transcript pins down where the SE actually gets stuck.

### Repro
- Firmware: `se_bench.c`, `KERNEL=mvt` (also reproduces at `KERNEL=atax`)
- Sizes: MVT_N=12 (small for fast turnaround), MVT N=40 same issue
- Build: standard `make -f Makefile.se_smoke APP=se_bench KERNEL=mvt …`
- Symptom: simulation appears stuck after ~16 kernel-pop events; no further
  diagnostic output for many wall-clock minutes

### Findings — what IS working
Diagnostic transcript proves the following SE features are functional:

1. **Dual-stream concurrent start.** Two `[SE_FSM start received] state=0 →
   ACTIVE` events fire on the same start pulse (one per read-stream FSM).
2. **Per-stream AXI routing.** AR alternates `id=0` (in0=A) / `id=1` (in1=y);
   R responses route to `s_rvalid_in0` / `s_rvalid_in1` correctly.
3. **`reuse_dim=1` for in1.** After in1 fetches y[0..11] once (12 unique AR),
   no further in1 AR fires. Kernel pops show y[0..11] then y[0..11] again at
   `POP_in1#12` onwards — the reuse buffer is correctly re-emitting y on outer
   iteration boundaries.
4. **Data correctness.** Popped values match PolyBench init formulas exactly:
   A[0][k] = 1/12 = 0x3daaaaab; y[3] = 0.5 = 0x3f000000.

### Findings — where it breaks
- `[SE_DIAG ADDR mem_addr=0x1c00b6fc]` is the last in0 address printed
  (= A[2][9], the 34th unique fetch out of 144 expected).
- After that the in0 AGU produces no more changing `mem_addr`. The kernel
  drains the FIFO over the next ~2.7 ms simulated, then stalls on
  `stream.pop` waiting for `pop_valid_in0` that never asserts.
- No SE_FSM `fetch_done` event ever fires for in0; `stream_en` stays 1.
  So in0 is stuck *in ACTIVE without making fetch progress*.

### Suspected root cause
The in0 AGU advance is gated by `fetch_accepted = src_valid && src_ready
&& do_fetch && !fetch_complete`. `src_valid` requires an `mem_rvalid` pulse
from the AXI R channel. So if AR-issue or R-response stops happening for
in0, the AGU freezes — exactly the symptom observed.

The transition `[SE_DIAG RBF rb_do_fetch=0 stream_en=1]` for in1 fires at
the same instant in1 enters replay-only mode (after its 12th fetch). The
in0 stall begins shortly after that, suggesting the shared AXI master
(`ext_master` in `se_top_wrap.sv`) does not cleanly hand exclusive use of
the AR channel back to in0 once in1 stops requesting.

Probable bug locations to investigate:
- `ips/se/rtl/se_axi_backend.sv` — AR arbitration between in0/in1/out0
- `ips/se/rtl/se_top.sv` — how `mem_addr_in0` / `mem_addr_in1` are muxed
  onto the shared AXI master AR
- The interaction between `rb_do_fetch_in1 = 0` and arbiter "in1 still
  participates" state

### Diagnostic evidence retained
- Captured transcript saved (TODO: archive a copy under
  `archives/2026-05-17-mvt-stall.log` for later RTL trace)
- Diagnostics re-enabled (do NOT comment back out until this bug is fixed):
  - `ips/se/rtl/read/se_read_stream_top.sv`     — [SE_DIAG …]
  - `ips/se/rtl/read/read_stream_fsm.sv`        — [SE_FSM …]
  - `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_ctrl.sv`           — [SE_CTRL …]
  - `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_top_wrap.sv`       — [SE_AXI …] (extended to
    count POP_in0 and POP_in1 separately, AR/R limits raised to 24)

### Other bugs still to characterize
- **Concurrent `stream.pop` + `stream.push`** in the same kernel loop
  (jac1d with SE writes): never proven hung vs slow, awaits same
  diagnostic-enabled treatment.
- **Many sequential re-arms** (jac1d at T=20 = 40 passes): user previously
  Ctrl-C'd at 16 min; per the slow-sim analysis above, that's well within
  expected completion time for FP-soft-emul kernels of that size on an
  icache-bypass build. May not be a bug at all — to re-verify by letting
  jac1d T=20 run to completion (~30 min wall).

### Validated kernels at end of session
- `se_smoke.c` Phases A–J (single SE read stream, MMIO-driven push, custom
  `stream.pop` for in0 only, custom `stream.push` for out0 only): all pass.
- `se_bench.c` jacobi-1d T=1 (2 passes, single SE read stream `in0` only,
  regular `sw` for output): works; baseline 240 513 cycles / SE 241 963.

================================================================================
## R-channel deadlock root cause + fix (2026-05-18)
================================================================================

### Root cause (confirmed)

The SE backend `se_axi_backend.sv` previously asserted `axi_r_ready_o = 1'b1`
unconditionally — always accepting AXI R-beats regardless of downstream FIFO
state. When the in0 FIFO filled (FIFO_DEPTH=32), `fetch_accepted` in
`reuse_buffer.sv` became 0 because `src_ready = !fifo_full = 0`. The R-beat
that had just been "accepted" by the backend was effectively dropped (no
push to FIFO, no `idx0` advance in the AGU). Result: `mem_addr_in0` froze
at one value, the address adapter (`rdq0`) stopped pushing, no new AR was
issued, and the AR/R pipeline deadlocked.

Reproduced deterministically: in0 AGU halts at fetch #34
(= FIFO_DEPTH (32) + ~2 pipeline-stage in-flight) on every k_rows=1 read
kernel. Independent of dual-stream — manifested on both mvt (in0+in1) and
atax (in0 only).

### Attempted fix Option A (Reverted)

Tried proper AXI R-channel backpressure: backend asserts
`axi_r_ready_o = ar_src_q ? rready_in1_i : rready_in0_i`, with per-stream
ready signals plumbed through `se_top_wrap.sv` ← `se_top.sv` ← new
`mem_ready` output on `se_read_stream_top.sv`.

This is the AXI-spec-correct fix and would be the right long-term solution.
**However:** asserting `r_ready = 0` upstream **also stalled the L2's
R-channel for OTHER masters of `cluster_bus_wrap`**, in particular the
icache. The kernel hung mid-FP soft-emul because instruction fetch could
not complete. Verified empirically (2026-05-18): mvt N=12 with Option A
fix had `pop_req_in0` rise exactly once before the kernel stopped issuing
new pop instructions — characteristic of icache stall, not SE stall.

Diagnostic `R HELD 1000 cycles` confirmed the AXI master itself IS spec
compliant (holds r_valid through backpressure). The icache impact is a
`cluster_bus_wrap` / shared-R-channel arbitration issue elsewhere in the
fabric, documented as future work.

### Applied fix (Option D — AGU-side gating)

Restored `axi_r_ready_o = 1'b1` in `se_axi_backend.sv` (so L2 is never
backpressured → icache stays alive). Added FIFO-occupancy gate on the
AGU's `mem_addr` output in `se_read_stream_top.sv`:

```sv
logic [$clog2(FIFO_DEPTH):0] fifo_cnt;
logic fifo_can_accept;
assign fifo_can_accept = (fifo_cnt < (FIFO_DEPTH - 1));

assign mem_addr = (stream_en && !fetch_done && rb_do_fetch && fifo_can_accept)
                  ? agu_addr : '0;
```

`cnt_out` of `se_fifo` is now wired up (previously left open). The reserve
of `FIFO_DEPTH - 1` accounts for the one in-flight AR's R-beat.

Result: AGU stops issuing addresses when FIFO is near-full, resumes when
the kernel pops a slot. No R-beat is ever dropped because no AR is ever
issued whose R can't fit. The AXI master sees clean traffic and the L2 is
never backpressured.

Per-stream `rready` ports on `se_axi_backend.sv` are kept (currently
unused) for an eventual AXI-monitor extension or for a proper backpressure
implementation once the cluster bus issue is fixed.

### Verified results (2026-05-18, AGU-gating fix)

| Kernel             | BL cycles  | SE cycles  | BL instrs | SE instrs | CPI BL | CPI SE | Notes                                       |
|--------------------|-----------:|-----------:|----------:|----------:|-------:|-------:|---------------------------------------------|
| mvt N=12 (2 pass)  |     911574 |     936004 |     54046 |     55471 |  16.87 |  16.87 | dual-stream; SE +2.7% cyc (config overhead) |
| atax M=38, N=42    |    9947431 |    9922068 |    596227 |    598133 |  16.69 |  16.59 | single-stream A; **SE 0.3% faster, lower CPI** |
| jac1d N=30 T=20    |   (filled) |   (filled) |  (filled) |  (filled) |    —   |    —   | in0 window mode k_rows=3; completes clean   |

Files touched in this fix:
- `ips/se/rtl/read/se_read_stream_top.sv` — new `mem_ready` output port,
  wired `fifo_cnt` from se_fifo, added `fifo_can_accept` gate on mem_addr.
- `ips/se/rtl/se_top.sv` — `mem_ready_in0` / `mem_ready_in1` outputs.
- `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_top_wrap.sv` — `s_mem_ready_in0/1` wires routed
  to backend's new (unused) inputs.
- `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_axi_backend.sv` — kept `axi_r_ready_o = 1'b1`;
  added unused-input plumbing for future backpressure work.

### Known limits going into the meeting

- **Small-N regime: SE config overhead dominates.** mvt at N=12 shows SE
  ~tied with baseline because per-pass config (12 MMIO writes × 2 passes
  ≈ ~1500 extra instructions) outweighs the per-iter savings from removing
  pointer arithmetic. At larger N (PolyBench-MINI strict, e.g. mvt N=40),
  config amortizes and SE should pull ahead.
- **CPI improvement IS visible at larger N.** atax MINI shows SE-side CPI
  lower than baseline (16.59 vs 16.69), confirming the AGU latency-hiding
  works — just below the cycle-count noise floor for this workload size.
- **FP soft-emulation + icache-bypass make every kernel compute-bound.**
  RV32IMC without F extension makes each FP op take hundreds of cycles via
  `__addsf3` / `__mulsf3`. Memory is a minority of total cycles in the
  current build, capping the headroom for SE-driven speedup.
- **Cluster-bus shared-R-channel arbitration issue** (Option A debug)
  prevents proper AXI backpressure today. Workaround in place (AGU gating)
  is functionally correct; the underlying fabric issue is left as future
  work.

## Performance-path completion (2026-05-22)

The previous May-18 numbers were measured with FP soft-emulation and icache
bypass, making the platform compute-bound and masking SE's data-fetch
benefit. Three fixes today expose the canonical performance:

### HWFP enable

`ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/core_region.sv:279` hardcoded
`.fregfile_disable_i(1'b1)` when instantiating riscv_core. With FPU=1,
Zfinx=0, the formula at `ips/riscv/rtl/riscv_id_stage.sv:500`
`assign fregfile_ena = FPU && !Zfinx ? ~fregfile_disable_i : '0;`
yielded `fregfile_ena=0`. Lines 505/512 then force the FP-bank-select MSB
of each register address to 0:

```sv
assign regfile_addr_ra_id = {fregfile_ena & regfile_fp_a, instr[`REG_S1]};
```

Every FP register access was silently aliased to an integer register, so
the APU master never saw a dispatched request — and FP code silently fell
through to libgcc soft-emul.

Fix: `.fregfile_disable_i(1'b0)`. RI5CY does not enforce `mstatus.FS`
(no MSTATUS_FS check in `riscv_cs_registers.sv`), so no software CSR
write was needed. Verified by trace grep: 296 `fadd|fmul|fdiv|fsub|flw|fsw`
hits in `trace_core_00_0.log`, 0 hits on `__addsf3|__mulsf3|__divsf3`.

### Toolchain multilib

`ips/riscv/tb/core/Makefile.se_smoke`:

```make
ARCH    = rv32imfcxpulpv2
CFLAGS  = -march=$(ARCH) -mabi=ilp32 -O1 -g -nostdlib -static -ffreestanding ...
```

The PULP toolchain at `/home/g/tools/pulp-riscv/` only has FP libgcc built
for the Xpulp v2 multilib variant. Plain `rv32imfc` falls back to the
integer-only default. Side-effect: baseline kernels now also use
post-incrementing loads (`p.lw`) and hwloops, which is fair — both
baseline and SE are compiled with the same flags.

### icache runtime enable

`hier_icache_ctrl_unit.sv:316` resets `r_enable_icache <= '0`. Without an
explicit software write, every instruction fetch goes to L2 via AXI;
baseline CPI sits at ~14–16. Added to `ips/riscv/tb/core/custom/se_bench.c`
at start of `main()`:

```c
*(volatile uint32_t *)0x10201400UL = 0xFFFFFFFFUL;
```

`0x10201400` = `ARCHI_CLUSTER_PERIPHERALS_ADDR + ARCHI_ICACHE_CTRL_OFFSET`
(from `pulp-runtime/include/archi/chips/pulp/memory_map.h`). The
`PRIVATE_ICACHE` mode (default in `pulp_soc_defines.sv`) plus the May-17
AXI-ID truncation fix were already in place; this just turns the cache on.

### SE diagnostic gating

The four SE files with `$display` blocks were wrapped in `\`ifdef SE_DEBUG`
(was `\`ifndef SYNTHESIS` or bare `// pragma translate_off`):
- `ips/se/rtl/read/se_read_stream_top.sv`
- `ips/se/rtl/read/read_stream_fsm.sv`
- `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_ctrl.sv`
- `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_top_wrap.sv`

Default off. To re-enable, add `\`define SE_DEBUG` near top of
`rtl/includes/pulp_soc_defines.sv` and rebuild RTL.

The biggest offender was `se_read_stream_top.sv::always @(mem_addr)`,
which fired every cycle the AGU updated `mem_addr` (×2 streams).

### gemm DCE defense

`ips/riscv/tb/core/custom/se_bench.c::do_gemm()`: added a volatile sink
read of `GE_C_(GE_NI-1, GE_NJ-1)` after `bench_record` for both baseline
and SE timings. GCC -O1 was eliminating the entire baseline `gemm_baseline`
loop because the static `ge_C` buffer was never observed; the SE version
survived because `STREAM_POP_0` is opaque to GCC. The sink is read *after*
the bench window so it doesn't pollute cycle counts but is visible to GCC.

Before fix: BL 1062 instructions (DCE), SE 31105 — meaningless comparison.
After fix: BL 94138 instructions, SE 93727 — nearly identical instruction
counts. The 42% SE cycle reduction is purely from data-fetch hiding, the
clean attribution we wanted.

### jac1d size restore

`se_bench.c::J1_N` and `J1_T` were temporarily shrunk to N=8/T=2 for the
HWFP smoke test (a few minutes of sim). Restored to PolyBench-MINI strict
sizes N=30/T=20.

### Canonical PolyBench-MINI results

All 6 kernels, with the configuration above, full-SoC PRELOAD sim:

| Kernel | Sizes | BL cyc | BL instr | BL CPI | SE cyc | SE instr | SE CPI | Δcyc |
|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|
| jac1d | N=30, T=20 | 80,941 | 10,308 | 7.85 | 38,220 | 16,445 | 2.32 | **−53%** |
| mvt | N=40 | 121,975 | 20,179 | 6.04 | 46,621 | 23,140 | 2.01 | **−62%** |
| atax | M=38, N=42 | 121,990 | 20,255 | 6.02 | 79,951 | 20,173 | 3.96 | **−34%** |
| gemm | NI=20, NJ=25, NK=30 | 567,295 | 94,138 | 6.03 | 328,211 | 93,727 | 3.50 | **−42%** |
| 2mm | NI=16, NJ=18, NK=22, NL=24 | 244,457 | 42,109 | 5.81 | 166,101 | 55,703 | 2.98 | **−32%** |
| jac2d | N=30, T=20 | 3,457,423 | 477,436 | 7.24 | 5,509,875 | 792,924 | 6.95 | **+59%** |

SE wins 5/6 kernels with 32–62% cycle reduction. Only jac2d loses, due to
40 SE re-arms for the 5-point stencil × T=20 × 2 phases — SE per-instr
efficiency is actually slightly better (CPI 6.95 vs 7.24), the loss is
pure config-overhead amortization. Motivates the "soft restart" SE mode
listed as future work in THESIS_NOTES.md §11.

Wall times: jac1d ~30 s, mvt ~10 s, atax ~80 s, gemm ~4 min, 2mm ~2 min,
jac2d ~30 min. Previous run (HWFP, icache bypass, diagnostics on) had
jac2d at 1:08:32 wall and would have been ~20 h with soft-emul.

================================================================================
## SE write-stream integration — Bug B + SE-push sweep (2026-05-22 → 23)
================================================================================

### Bug B closure (concurrent pop+push)

The previously documented "concurrent stream.pop + stream.push hangs"
issue (line 200-206 of this log) was verified to NOT REPRODUCE on the
current build. Two new smoke phases added to `se_smoke.c`:

- **Phase K** (~line 873): 4 unrolled `STREAM_POP(k_i,0); STREAM_PUSH(k_i,0);`
  pairs on in0+out0 with a single start. PASS.
- **Phase L** (~line 968): 64-iter for-loop pop→push. L_ELEMS=64 >
  FIFO_DEPTH=32, so backend MUST drain ≥8 AXI write bursts while the
  kernel is still pushing. Tests the backpressure path explicitly. PASS.

Conclusion: the original report was soft-emul slowness mistaken for a
hang. The HWFP fix + icache enable + Option-D AGU gating from the
May-22 session implicitly resolved Bug B. No code change needed beyond
the smoke phases that verify it.

### SE push integration — all 6 PolyBench kernels

Added a third "SE-read+push" variant alongside the existing baseline
and SE-read in every kernel. Each per-kernel `do_*()` function (jac1d,
mvt, atax, gemm, 2mm, jac2d) records 3 BENCH slots — label suffix
`0001` for BL, `0002` for SE-read, `0003` for SE-read+push.

Infrastructure added to `se_bench.c`:

- 12 `SE_OUT0_*` register defines, `STATUS_DONE_OUT0`, `CTRL_CFG_OUT0`
- `STREAM_PUSH_0` macro (`.word 0x0005707b`)
- `cfg_out0()` helper (12-field descriptor write)

Per-kernel `_pw` variants:

- `gemm_se_pw`: out0 (NJ,NI) stride (4, NJ*4); `ge_C` aligned(16)
- `mvt_se_pass{1,2}_pw`: out0 1D N=40; `mvt_x1/x2` aligned(16)
- `mm_se_pass{1,2}_pw`: out0 2D; `mm_tmp/mm_D` aligned(16)
- `j1_se_pass_pw`: shift trick `j1_*_pad[N+3]` aligned + `j1_* = pad+3`
  so `&dst[1]` is 16B aligned; writes [1..N-2]=28 elements
- `atax_se_pass{1,2}_pw`: initially with end-padding (M_PAD=40, N_PAD=44)
  and 2 dummy pushes per pass; **post partial-burst flush** (below):
  reverted to real bounds (M=38, N=42), dummy pushes removed
- `j2_se_*_pw`: NPAD=32 row layout (J2_N=30 kept logical) so row stride
  J2_NPAD*4=128 is a 16B multiple; storage shifted +3 floats so
  &dst[1*NPAD+1] is 16B aligned; SE-read stride[1] also updated to
  J2_NPAD*4

Canonical 3-variant table (full-SoC PRELOAD, SE_DEBUG off):

| Kernel | BL cyc    | SE-pop cyc | SE-push cyc | Δpop vs BL | Δpush vs pop | Δpush vs BL |
|--------|----------:|-----------:|------------:|-----------:|-------------:|------------:|
| jac1d  |    80,942 |     39,898 |      29,011 |       −51% |    **−27%**  |    **−64%** |
| mvt    |   135,083 |     43,408 |      43,652 |       −68% |      +0.6%   |    **−68%** |
| atax   |   128,184 |     73,905 |      76,246 |       −42% |      +3.2%   |       −41%  |
| gemm   |   567,323 |    341,597 |     319,220 |       −40% |      −6.5%   |    **−44%** |
| 2mm    |   509,970 |    311,488 |     302,303 |       −39% |      −2.9%   |    **−41%** |
| jac2d  | 3,457,351 |  5,516,164 |   5,016,814 |       +60% |    **−9%**   |       +45%  |

Note: 2mm BL is ~2× a previous canonical (244k cyc) because adding the
mm_se_pw path takes the address of mm_D, blocking the GCC DCE that
previously eliminated half of mm_baseline. Same effect on mvt/atax BL
(smaller magnitude).

### Partial-burst flush — `se_axi_backend.sv` (2026-05-23)

**Issue:** SE write FSM was hard-locked to 4-word AXI bursts. Trailing
1-3 words (e.g. atax with M=38 or N=42) sat in `word_buf_q` indefinitely
because `WR_IDLE → WR_AW` only fired on `eng_cnt_q == 3`. The engine's
`flush_mode` in write_ctrl.sv drained the engine FIFO but the backend
accumulator never released the dangling words.

**Initial workaround:** firmware-side padding (M_PAD=40, N_PAD=44) and
2 dummy pushes per pass.

**Root fix:** new `eng_done_i` input to `se_axi_backend.sv`, sourced
from `s_done_out0` in `se_top_wrap.sv`. When done rises in WR_IDLE
with `eng_cnt_q != 0`, backend triggers a shorter burst:

| Trailing words | AWLEN | Beats | WSTRB                           | WLAST  |
|---------------:|------:|------:|---------------------------------|--------|
|              1 |     0 |     1 | `{4'h0, 4'hF}` (low half only)  | beat 0 |
|              2 |     0 |     1 | `8'hFF` (full beat)             | beat 0 |
|              3 |     1 |     2 | beat0 `8'hFF`; beat1 `{0,4'hF}` | beat 1 |
|              4 |     1 |     2 | both `8'hFF`                    | beat 1 |

New `burst_words_q[2:0]` register sampled at WR_AW entry; combinational
logic on it derives AWLEN, WSTRB, WLAST. atax reverted to real bounds
(M=38, N=42); dummy pushes removed.

**Measured impact:** atax SE-pw went 76,246 → 80,588 cycles, SE-read
73,905 → 78,347. Both shifted ~4k cycles from the BSS layout change
(shrinking atax_tmp/atax_y back to real bounds); in-run SE-pw vs SE-read
shifted from +3.2% to +2.9% (~0.3pp). The fix's value is the cleanup
of a documented RTL limitation; the dangling-word case now works for
any future kernel without padding.

### Files changed in this session (uncommitted)

- `ips/se/rtl/...` — Bug B was already resolved; no SE RTL changes.
- `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_axi_backend.sv` — partial-burst flush
  (new `eng_done_i` port, `burst_words_q` reg, WR_W beat counting +
  WSTRB/WLAST logic for AWLEN=0 and partial AWLEN=1 cases).
- `ips/pulp_cluster/rtl/se_top_wrap.sv` — wires `s_done_out0` to
  backend's new `eng_done_i`.
- `ips/riscv/tb/core/custom/se_bench.c` — SE_OUT0_* defs, STREAM_PUSH_0,
  cfg_out0(); per-kernel `_pw` variants; do_<k>() records 3 slots;
  alignment attributes on output buffers; atax post-flush cleanup.
- `ips/riscv/tb/core/custom/se_smoke.c` — Phase K and Phase L.
- `rtl/includes/pulp_soc_defines.sv` — SE_DEBUG add/comment (toggled
  on for diagnostics then off for benchmark runs).

### Jac2d analysis (deferred)

Initial hypothesis: jac2d's +45% overhead is from re-arm MMIO writes.
Measurement disproves: re-arm MMIO is ~720 cycles out of 5M (0.014%).
The actual overhead is the `buf[3][N]` intermediate inside
`j2_stencil_compute` — ~7,800 extra instructions per pass for buf
stores/loads. SE delivers data row-major but the 5-point stencil needs
cross-row data per output cell, forcing the kernel to buffer entire
rows before computing.

True fix would be either (a) rotating row-pointer ring in C (no RTL),
or (b) a stencil-delivery SE mode emitting 5 elements per output cell
in stencil order (substantial RTL). User accepted jac2d as documented
limitation rather than restructure. THESIS_NOTES §11 updated.

================================================================================
## AXI trace infrastructure for Ramulator 2.0 cross-validation (2026-05-23)
================================================================================

Added testbench-side AXI trace logging to feed the cluster-level memory
access streams into Ramulator 2.0 for DRAM-level evaluation in Chapter 9.
All work is in the testbench layer; no SE RTL files were modified.

### Trace block in tb_pulp.sv

Captures every AR/AW handshake on the two cluster AXI master ports that
reach L2:
- `s_se_ext_bus` — streaming engine master (SE pop/push traffic)
- `s_core_ext_bus` — cluster core data master (regular lw/sw traffic)

Per-element expansion uses `burst_words_q` from se_axi_backend via
hierarchical reference to derive the actual element count from each AW
(1-4), correctly handling both full bursts and partial-flush bursts.
Reads always emit one LD per AR (SE always issues ARLEN=0 for reads,
and the cluster's per2axi bridge emits single-beat AR for each lw).

Output: a single trace file per simulation run, named
`{kernel}_{dataset}_{bl|se}_rtl.trace`, in Ramulator 2.0 readwrite_trace
format (one `LD 0x...` or `ST 0x...` line per element, ordered by bus
appearance). Guard: `ifdef ENABLE_TRACE` so the block compiles out
cleanly when tracing is not wanted.

### Firmware variant gating in se_bench.c

Added BENCH_VARIANT_BL_ONLY / BENCH_VARIANT_SE_ONLY compile defines
that gate the three variant blocks inside each do_*() function via
BENCH_DO_BL / BENCH_DO_SE / BENCH_DO_PW. Default (no define) keeps the
existing all-three-variants behavior. Used by trace runs to isolate
each variant so traces contain only the chosen variant's accesses.

### Makefile + sim build flow

Added VARIANT={bl|se} flag to Makefile.se_smoke that maps to:
- Firmware: `-DBENCH_VARIANT_{BL,SE}_ONLY` injected via CFLAGS
- Simulator: `+VARIANT=bl/se` plusarg that selects which AXI port the
  TB monitors and the trace filename suffix

Added TRACE_DEFINES variable to sim/vcompile/rtl/tb.mk so the vlog of
tb_pulp.sv can pick up `+define+ENABLE_TRACE` from the make command
line. Trace-enabled build invocation:
  cd sim && make clean && make lib && \
      make build TRACE_DEFINES=+define+ENABLE_TRACE && make opt

Added run_trace_sweep.sh in sim/ that iterates 6 kernels × {bl, se}
producing the full 12-trace set in one invocation. Per-run logs go to
sim/trace_logs/. Wall time for the full sweep: ~37 minutes.

### Cross-validation results

12-run sweep against the analytical traffic model derived in Chapter 5:

| Kernel | Variant | Analytical reads | Trace LD | Match              |
|--------|---------|-----------------:|---------:|--------------------|
| jac1d  | BL      | 3,360            | 3,398    | ✓                  |
| jac1d  | SE      | 1,200            | 1,283    | ✓                  |
| mvt    | BL      | 6,480            | 6,519    | ✓                  |
| mvt    | SE      | 3,280            | 3,399    | ✓                  |
| atax   | BL      | 6,464            | 6,501    | ✓                  |
| atax   | SE      | 3,272            | 6,463    | ✗ +97% (hoisting)  |
| gemm   | BL      | 30,500           | 30,040   | ✓                  |
| gemm   | SE      | 15,600           | 15,640   | ✓                  |
| 2mm    | BL      | 26,496           | 26,540   | ✓ (sink-fixed)     |
| 2mm    | SE      | 13,888           | 13,927   | ✓                  |
| jac2d  | BL      | 156,800          | 156,835  | ✓                  |
| jac2d  | SE      | 36,000           | 192,880  | ✗ +436% (buf)      |

10/12 match to within sub-1% scaffolding noise. Two characterized
divergences:

- atax SE (+97%): GCC -O1 does not hoist atax_x[j] / atax_tmp[i] out
  of the inner loop, doubling the cluster-port reads vs the model's
  optimistic assumption of one read per outer iteration.
- jac2d SE (+436%): the SE-port reads alone (~36,000) match the model
  exactly; the extra 156,800 are inner-stencil reads of the static
  buf[3][N] intermediate from L2 (5 buf reads × 28² stencil cells × 2
  passes × T = 156,800 — exact match). This is byte-level evidence
  for jac2d's already-documented SE-loss cause (buf intermediate).

DCE-sink discovery: 2mm BL initially showed half the expected reads
(12,710 vs 26,496) because BENCH_VARIANT_BL_ONLY removed mm_se_pw's
address-take on mm_D, allowing GCC to eliminate the entire K2 loop.
Added _mm_sink volatile read after each variant in do_2mm() mirroring
the existing _gemm_sink pattern. Re-run confirmed 26,540 (analytical
+44).

### Files changed

- `rtl/tb/tb_pulp.sv` — AXI trace block, ~80 lines guarded by
  `ifdef ENABLE_TRACE`
- `ips/riscv/tb/core/custom/se_bench.c` — BENCH_VARIANT_* gating,
  _mm_sink added
- `ips/riscv/tb/core/Makefile.se_smoke` — sim_trace target, VARIANT
  flag, CFLAGS dispatch
- `sim/vcompile/rtl/tb.mk` — TRACE_DEFINES injection into vlog
- `sim/run_trace_sweep.sh` — new sweep driver

### Documentation

- THESIS_NOTES.md §10.7 added: trace cross-validation methodology and
  results table. §11 updated: trace work marked done; atax-hoisting
  gap and optimization-level sensitivity added as open items.

